00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.610
And when somebody
says hello, I'm

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:43.000
going to use the repository
to also save these records.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.590
I'm going to say save person.

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:46.578
Name is the name, and
something like that.

00:09:46.578 --> 00:09:48.370
Now of course, that is
probably not enough.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.980
You probably want to make
a call to another service.

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:52.780
I don't have time to
write another service

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:53.630
for that matter.

00:09:53.630 --> 00:09:55.007
However, I can simulate one.

00:09:55.007 --> 00:09:56.590
For that matter,
what I'm going to say

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:58.470
is I'm going to say
hello to everyone.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:00.400
I'm going to say hello
to a few people--

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.320
myself, Mandy, one
of the first person

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:05.920
I worked with when I joined
Google Cloud, and Jisha, who

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.390
was one of the engineers
who worked on this project

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:09.730
called Spring Cloud GCP.

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:11.680
And I see Ludo in the
audience over there,

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.590
so I'm going to say
hello to Ludo as well.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.340
And what this will do
is to basically make

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:20.800
a URL, a REST call, to
the Hello endpoint, going

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:24.060
through all of these names, and
say hello to each person, OK?

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.640
And in order for me to do
this, I do need to have,

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:28.380
in Spring's terms,
a REST template.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.730
So I do need to have
a little being here

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:34.580
that allows me to make
this request a lot easier.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:36.850
I'm going to go ahead and
use the interaction here.

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:39.370
I'm going to say private
val REST template.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.280
So that is automatically
injected into my controller.

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:44.970
And finally, I'm going
to say hello to everyone.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:45.880
Whew.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:47.560
So let's give it a try.

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:49.667
I'm going to go ahead and
go back to my terminal.

00:10:49.667 --> 00:10:50.500
Where's my terminal?

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:51.250
Right here.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:55.720
I'm going to stop this,
rerun this, and do a--

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:56.680
skip test.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:59.560
Maybe do a package
and Spring Boot run.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:03.130
So now I just went from a CRUD
service to another service

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:04.270
where I can say hello to.

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:06.340
It's going to save
the data and also

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:08.715
something else that's going
to say hello to three or four

00:11:08.715 --> 00:11:10.720
different people.

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:12.080
So let's see how this works out.

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:13.630
So everything started.

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:16.730
For this one, I think I'm going
to go to my console instead.

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:19.330
So I'm going to do
this rather, because I

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:21.100
like this view a little better.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:24.660
So I'm going to go ahead and
say curl localhost 8080 hello.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:26.440
And name is--

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:27.700
I think Tamao in the audience.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:30.740
I'm going to say hello to
Tamao, who's from Weave.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:31.660
And there we go.

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:33.190
So I can say hello to Tamao.

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:35.800
And there we go.

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:39.560
We can also see the
info log message here.

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:41.680
So now, let me
try to do the all.

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:44.530
Let me say, rather than
doing the curl localhost

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:47.442
with the hello, let me
just do all like that.

00:11:47.442 --> 00:11:49.650
And I should be able to say
hello to four people now,

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:50.230
right?

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:51.820
And every time I
say hello, remember,

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:53.570
it's saved into my Datastore.

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:55.240
So for that matter,
if I come back here

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:58.590
and do a little
refresh, this is real.

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.280
None of this is fake.

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:02.320
None of this is
premade, all right?

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:03.580
So then what do we do?

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:06.015
If you're making a call
to another microservice,

00:12:06.015 --> 00:12:08.650
in this kind of
architecture, all visibility

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:09.530
is very important.

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:10.947
What that means
is that you should

00:12:10.947 --> 00:12:12.830
be able to see one
call to another.

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:15.280
Via HTTP or messaging
or whatever,

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:17.830
you should be able to see
how the messages are flowing.

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:20.020
To do that, we have a
really, really easy way

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:23.150
in Spring, and especially
with our integration.

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:25.240
We can actually use
Stackdriver Trace.

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:28.300
And all we need to do is, by
adding this one dependency,

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.860
we will automatically
hook into your application

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:32.830
and we know where
the request came in.

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:34.450
We know what headers
to propagate.

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:36.640
And now we can have
Trace propagated

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:39.167
to Stackdriver automatically.

00:12:39.167 --> 00:12:40.750
Furthermore, what
I'm also going to do

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:43.060
is by adding a
logging dependency

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:45.040
here so that all
of the log messages

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:48.040
can actually go directly
into a Stackdriver Logging.

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:51.490
And something happens after
that really, really cool.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:53.890
Now, because this is a demo--

00:12:53.890 --> 00:13:02.100
so typically, when you call a
simple every single request,

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:04.210
you don't really trace
every single request.

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:06.250
But because this is a
demo, what we need to do

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:08.270
is to actually trace everything.

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:11.530
So for that matter, I'm going
to up the probability of a trace

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.030
to about 100%.

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:13.840
So here we go.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.500
I'm going to say probability
of a trace is 100%.

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:18.710
I'm going to trace
100% of the requests.

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:20.500
Now finally, the last
thing I need to do

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:22.210
is to configure my logger.

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:25.640
To do that in Spring, they
use logback by default.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:29.163
So I'm going to create a
new logback configuration.

00:13:29.163 --> 00:13:30.830
And what I can do
here is to, of course,

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:32.050
add a logback configuration.

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:34.370
I do have one here
for API and console.

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:35.830
So what this one
does is that it's

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:39.250
going to log to the console so I
can see it on my local machine.

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:42.100
But I'm also going to send
the same log of messages

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:44.410
to Stackdriver Logging as well.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:47.710
So I'm going to go ahead and
clear this and restart and run.

00:13:47.710 --> 00:13:51.430
Now, we're about 15 minutes in.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:53.530
We will see how this works out.

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:55.240
So I'm skipping tests.

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:59.280
Best practices ever for a demo.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:00.310
And we'll see.

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:01.957
So-- oh.

00:14:01.957 --> 00:14:03.790
Yeah, there's a little
error, but that's OK.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:04.790
That's kind of expected.

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:06.280
That's expected error exception.

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:09.430
And we're going to fix that in
the future releases of this.

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:12.170
But we should be
able to see 8080.

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:13.190
I'm going to say hello.

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:14.270
Name is DevZone.

00:14:14.270 --> 00:14:16.120
That's where we are right now.

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:18.480
And I'm going to say
hello to everyone as well.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:23.410
So I'm going to say hello
curl localhost 8080/all.

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.600
Now, one thing that
you may have noticed

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:27.730
is that in the log
messages, not only

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:31.030
do we see the log,
the message itself.

00:14:31.030 --> 00:14:33.430
What we also see
are these weird IDs.

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:34.690
These are Trace IDs.

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:37.300
Actually, the first part
of it is the Trace ID.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:39.580
And the second part is
the [? Spring ?] ID.

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:43.330
And these IDs are automatically
captured by our Trace starter

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:45.160
and propagated to
the log messages.

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:51.340
What that also means is that,
if I go back to my Trace console

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:53.590
for Stackdriver
Trace, I should be

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:56.830
able to see that particular
request that just came through.

00:14:56.830 --> 00:14:59.080
And I can probably see it here.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:01.900
So I can see I
said hello to all.

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:05.110
I can see that I said hello
to Ray, Mandy, and Jisha,

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:06.490
and Ludo.

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:07.690
But here's the best part.

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:09.970
If you click on the little
thing called Show log.

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:12.160
You can see all the
log messages correlated

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:14.680
to each of the spans,
each of the Trace.

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.492
This is super, super
useful when you're

00:15:16.492 --> 00:15:17.950
trying to debug
and figure out what

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:21.050
happened to your application
in a production environment.

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:22.710
Now, with the last
four minutes, I'm

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.098
going to go ahead and
deploy this into App Engine.

00:15:25.098 --> 00:15:27.640
If you don't know App Engine,
it's the platform as a service.

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:29.520
In my mind, it is the
original serverless

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:31.270
because you can scale
out your application

00:15:31.270 --> 00:15:33.460
to however many
instances you need.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:35.590
You also scale back
in to zero when you

00:15:35.590 --> 00:15:37.060
don't have any more requests.

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:40.315
So we have this tool
called the Cloud Code.

00:15:40.315 --> 00:15:42.890
It used to be called GCP Tools.

00:15:42.890 --> 00:15:45.640
But this is a plugin
you can add to your IDE.

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:48.280
I'm going to go ahead and
add App Engine support here.

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:50.530
And what this will do is to
add a little configuration

00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:51.760
file for me.

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:54.980
So I can go ahead
and deploy my app.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:55.480
All right.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:58.307
So we added this thing
called the appengine-web.xml.

00:15:58.307 --> 00:16:00.640
Now of course, with the newer
version of the App Engine,

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:03.580
we can run Java 11, as
well as the JAR file.

00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:05.020
But just because
of the demo, I'm

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:06.870
going to go ahead and
up the instance size.

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:08.470
I'm going to do a
manual scaling just

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:11.430
so I know I definitely have
one instance running somewhere.

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:16.050
And then I'm going to use the
IDE to actually do the deploy.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:19.030
So let me see if I
can get to IDE here.

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:19.660
All right.

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:22.340
So from the tool, I can go ahead
and configure my App Engine

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:23.410
deployment as well.

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:26.010
So I'm going to go ahead
and add in my configuration.

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:27.350
Now, this is all within the IDE.

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:29.320
This is all within
the Cloud Code IDE.

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:31.320
I'm going to go
ahead deploy my app

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:37.590
into my awesomely named
project called Wise Coyote.

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:39.970
And then I can give
it a version name.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:42.270
I'm going to call it
the version of Dev Zone.

00:16:42.270 --> 00:16:45.280
Maybe I promote it, maybe not.

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:46.670
Let's not promote it for now.

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:48.420
So I'm going to go
ahead and do this.

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:50.610
And I'm going to click Run.

00:16:50.610 --> 00:16:54.360
And this will actually deploy
my application, all packaged up,

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:55.840
uploading only the
files that have

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:57.710
been changed into App Engine.

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:00.510
And we're going to provision
the computing instances for you

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:03.450
behind the scenes, the
computing instances of this app.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:06.270
And we're also going to
give you an HTTPS endpoint.

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:10.230
And it will just be ready
to go and ready to be used.

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:13.319
And over here, you can see
that we also have the URL.

00:17:13.319 --> 00:17:15.300
So this is a
devzone-dot-wise-coyote

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.609
whatever apps.com.

00:17:17.609 --> 00:17:21.579
And then down here
is the default URL.

00:17:21.579 --> 00:17:22.079
Oh.

00:17:22.079 --> 00:17:22.579
I see.

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:24.970
I think I did make
a mistake here.

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:27.510
I do need to use the B4
instance rather than F4.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:30.150
So I'm going to go ahead
and change that and do

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:31.600
the deployment again.

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:32.220
All right.

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.137
So it's going to go
through this motion again.

00:17:34.137 --> 00:17:36.940
It's going to upload whatever
files I changed and go ahead

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:39.450
and provision this
environment for me.

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:42.120
This may take a little time,
probably about 20 minutes

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:42.785
or so.

00:17:42.785 --> 00:17:43.660
No, I'm just kidding.

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:44.920
No.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:47.250
It should probably
take a minute max.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:48.840
But we will see what happened.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:49.560
Oh look.

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:51.570
Because I already
uploaded the files

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:54.000
in the previous
failed deployment,

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:55.710
the second deployment,
it actually

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:57.790
knows I didn't change any files.

00:17:57.790 --> 00:18:00.020
So it uploaded
zero files for me.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:02.670
It's really, really intelligent
and smart like that.

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:04.840
So now my application
is up and running.

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:07.140
I should be able to
go ahead and see it.

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:08.520
Oh my goodness.

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:11.190
I have, like, one minute left.

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:15.030
And now we're just waiting for
the application to boot up.

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:18.570
And eventually, eventually we
can actually see it, right?

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:21.390
So this is actually running
in a production environment.

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:23.160
I can see the Persons endpoint.

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:25.740
I can see everything
that I just created.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.110
And this is running
in App Engine in prod.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:30.540
Now, here's the last
thing I want to show you.

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:32.580
Let's assume something
bad happened.

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:35.400
It happens to me all the
time when I'm live coding.

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:37.860
We can actually debug
this application live.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:42.350
We can actually go to
Stackdriver Debugger.

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:46.400
And we should be able to find
my application here, which

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:48.170
is the Dev Zone version of it.

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:51.650
I can actually attach my
application, my source code

00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:54.110
into this application.

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:55.420
Upload it.

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:59.150
And then have you ever wondered,
hey, what did that user enter?

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:01.700
What was the input
string or whatever?

00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:04.040
If you want to find out,
with the proper permissions

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:05.600
and stuff, you can.

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:08.090
So what we can add is what
we call a snapshot point.

00:19:08.090 --> 00:19:10.370
So I can add a little
snapshot point here.

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:11.570
This is not a breakpoint.

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:12.920
It would not stop the world.

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:15.300
It would just capture the
state of the application

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:17.600
as the code flows through it.

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:19.570
So to show you this
last thing, I'm

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.220
going to go ahead
and do it this way.

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:24.740
One second.

00:19:24.740 --> 00:19:26.320
So I'm going to
go to my devzone.

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:29.210
I'm going to say
hello to someone.

00:19:29.210 --> 00:19:30.378
Anyone?

00:19:30.378 --> 00:19:30.920
I don't know.

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:33.260
I'm going to say hello
to Gloria, for example.

00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:36.020
Now, watch the
application on the right.

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:37.220
Did you see it?

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:38.300
Was it too fast?

00:19:38.300 --> 00:19:40.950
As soon as the application
went through that line of code,

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:42.390
we captured the state.

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:44.900
If we go into it, we can
see all the variables

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:47.570
that was in context of
that particular call.

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:50.340
I can also see all
of the code stack.

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:53.070
And this is a very, very
easy way to troubleshoot.

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:57.250
Now lastly, if you ever
forgot a log message,

00:19:57.250 --> 00:19:58.100
this is what you do.

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:00.360
You go to log point,
and you can just

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:03.110
do what I like to
call binary debugging.

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:03.860
Why?

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:04.700
With log messages.

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:06.140
Because this is what people do.

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:09.430
You say, first of all,
I'm starting from here.

00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:11.690
And then you say, well,
if everything works,

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:13.730
I should end here.

00:20:13.730 --> 00:20:16.470
And if it doesn't get there,
you put something in the middle.

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.137
That's why it's called
binary debugging.

00:20:18.137 --> 00:20:23.640
So at 1.5, maybe
something went wrong.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:25.790
Now, every single time
that the application

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:28.700
goes through these lines
of code, these log messages

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:32.300
will also be added to
your log automatically

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:34.610
without you having to report.

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:36.140
So that's all the time I got.

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:38.180
And if you want to
see more of this,

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:40.760
you can join my session,
a full 50-minute session

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:46.310
on all of these things and
more, tomorrow at 2:00 PM.

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:49.120
All right, so thank you
so much for being here.

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:49.720
Cheers.

